{% set phases = [
  {
    n: "1",
    name: "Kickoff and requirements",
    own: "The scope, the milestone plan, and the full list of systems that will feed the platform.",
    with: "The customer's commercial lead, their IT or data contact, and our account CSM.",
    out: "A scoped plan: every data source, every integration, the go-live date, and who owns each piece.",
    risk: "The goal arrives as a feature (\"we want segments\") instead of a result, and the system list is missing the one nobody mentions. I ask what they will do with the data on day one, and ask for the system list in writing, including the systems \"nobody uses anymore\"."
  },
  {
    n: "2",
    name: "Data mapping and integration design",
    own: "The technical meetings with each provider, the integration method, and the data schema.",
    with: "Ticketing, e-commerce and CRM providers, the customer's IT, and our developers when something new has to be built.",
    out: "A specification per source: endpoints, fields, schema, the logic that derives anything the source does not send, and calls I have already tested.",
    risk: "Documentation is missing or wrong, or an endpoint does not return what the commercial goal needs. I test the endpoints myself before a developer touches them, and when data is missing I add logic to the schema to derive it rather than wait for the provider."
  },
  {
    n: "3",
    name: "Build and configuration",
    own: "Standard integrations from the library, the per-customer data warehouse configuration, and the identity resolution design.",
    with: "Developers, only for integrations that do not exist yet.",
    out: "Data flowing on schedule and contacts resolving into single customer profiles.",
    risk: "Credentials and access arrive late, which is the most common reason a date slips, and identity resolution merges too much or too little. I track credentials as a dependency from the kickoff, and check profile counts on the first load against each source's own numbers."
  },
  {
    n: "4",
    name: "Testing and go-live",
    own: "Validation against the source systems and the go-live call.",
    with: "The customer's data owner, who signs off the numbers.",
    out: "Reconciled counts, a signed-off setup, and a live account.",
    risk: "The first dashboard disagrees with a report the customer already trusts, and confidence goes with it. I reconcile against their own reports before they see anything, and explain the differences, such as duplicates merged, before they have to ask."
  },
  {
    n: "5",
    name: "Handover and adoption",
    own: "Analytics dashboards, segment training, and the handover to whoever runs the account day to day.",
    with: "The customer's marketing and CRM team.",
    out: "Working dashboards, a team that builds its own target segments, and a documented setup.",
    risk: "The platform is live and nobody uses it. Training is built on their own goals, so the first segments they create in the session are ones they will actually send."
  }
] %}
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">How I run an <span class="text-brand-accent">enterprise onboarding</span></h1>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-lg text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
            <p class="mb-4">
                Most onboardings I lead take a sports club, league or federation onto a
                <strong class="text-brand-text">Customer Data Platform (CDP)</strong>: their ticketing, e-commerce, CRM
                and app data, merged into one profile per fan. I have worked on more than forty of them across 13 countries in Europe
                and the US, usually with a few running at once.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-4">
                Each phase below shows what I own, who I bring in, what it produces, and what usually goes wrong
                there, with how I catch it before go-live.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <ol class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-8 mb-16">
        {% for p in phases %}
        <li class="bg-brand-surface rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-brand-border p-6 md:p-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-5 flex items-center gap-4">
                <span class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-brand-accent text-brand-bg flex items-center justify-center text-lg font-bold shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">{{ p.n }}</span>
                <span><span class="sr-only">Phase {{ p.n }}: </span>{{ p.name }}</span>
            </h2>
            <dl class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <div>
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-brand-text uppercase tracking-wider text-xs mb-1">What I own</dt>
                    <dd class="text-brand-muted">{{ p.own }}</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-brand-text uppercase tracking-wider text-xs mb-1">Who I bring in</dt>
                    <dd class="text-brand-muted">{{ p.with }}</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="md:col-span-2">
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-brand-text uppercase tracking-wider text-xs mb-1">What it produces</dt>
                    <dd class="text-brand-muted">{{ p.out }}</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="md:col-span-2 border-l-4 border-brand-accent pl-4">
                    <dt class="font-semibold text-brand-text uppercase tracking-wider text-xs mb-1">What goes wrong, and how I catch it</dt>
                    <dd class="text-brand-muted">{{ p.risk }}</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ol>

    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-16">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">What carries over to the next one</h2>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-brand-muted leading-relaxed space-y-4">
            <p>
                My onboarding playbook started as a step-by-step framework in Jira, so nothing was forgotten, and
                grew into standard analytics setups, integration
                methods and mapping schemas. With the integrations library that followed, a known integration now
                takes hours to set up instead of the four to six weeks an onboarding took when I started.
            </p>
            <p>
                The latest layer is an AI toolkit I built on a knowledge library of how the platform is configured.
                It migrated customer accounts to a new segmentation engine through the portal's API, and it now
                builds dashboards and analytics tables from a customer's raw data sources.
                <a href="/portfolio/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">The case studies</a>
                show the method on real projects.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mt-8">
            <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">{% icon "fa-solid-envelope", "mr-2" %} Get in touch</a>
            <a href="/about/" class="btn btn-secondary">Read the full profile</a>
        </div>
    </section>
